### Install React 19

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/blog/2024/04/25/react-19-upgrade-guide.md

Official npm commands to install React 19 and its TypeScript types

```bash
npm install --save-exact react@^19.0.0 react-dom@^19.0.0
```

--------------------------------

### Full-Stack React Framework Recommendations

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/learn/creating-a-react-app.md

Official React team guidance on choosing frameworks and build tool integration for React applications. Specifically recommends starting with a framework (Next.js, React Router, Expo) that integrate the latest React features and architecture, supporting both client-side and server-side rendering strategies.

```markdown
// Production-grade frameworks supporting TypeScript and full-stack React:
// - Next.js (App Router) - full-stack React framework
// - React Router (v7) - client routing paired with Vite
// - Expo - for native apps (iOS, Android, web)

// These frameworks integrate:
// - React Server Components specification
// - Suspense for data fetching
// - Client-side rendering (CSR), Single-page apps (SPA), Static-site generation (SSG)
// - Optional server-side rendering on a per-route basis
```

--------------------------------

### TypeScript with React Components - Props Typing

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/learn/typescript.md

Official React guidance on typing React components in TypeScript, showing how to provide types for component props using interfaces and inline syntax. Covers the recommended pattern of using interfaces for reusable component specifications.

```typescript
interface MyButtonProps {
  /** The text to display inside the button */
  title: string;
  /** Whether the button can be interacted with */
  disabled: boolean;
}

function MyButton({ title, disabled }: MyButtonProps) {
  return (
    <button disabled={disabled}>{title}</button>
  );
}
```

--------------------------------

### Update react, react-dom, and react-server-dom-webpack together

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/blog/2025/12/03/critical-security-vulnerability-in-react-server-components.md

For a patch/security release, react, react-dom, and react-server-dom-webpack must all be updated together to the same version line. All three packages share a compatible exact-version dependency.

```bash
npm install react@latest react-dom@latest react-server-dom-webpack@latest
```

--------------------------------

### Create Next.js App

Source: https://github.com/reactjs/react.dev/blob/main/src/content/learn/creating-a-react-app.md

Official command to create a new Next.js app with App Router (React's recommended framework for full-stack React apps)

```bash
npx create-next-app@latest
```

### Next.js v16 주요 변경사항 및 기능

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/02-guides/upgrading/version-16.mdx

v16의 핵심 변경사항: Turbopack 기본 번들러, React 19.2 (View Transitions, useEffectEvent, Activity), React Compiler 안정화, Node.js 20.9+ 필요, Async Request APIs 필수, 캐싱 API (cacheLife, cacheTag, use cache), Middleware에서 Proxy로 이름 변경 등.

```markdown
## Turbopack by default

Starting with **Next.js 16**, Turbopack is stable and used by default with `next dev` and `next build`

## React 19.2

The App Router in **Next.js 16** uses the latest React [Canary release](https://react.dev/blog/2023/05/03/react-canaries), which includes the newly released React 19.2 features and other features being incrementally stabilized. Highlights include:

- **[View Transitions](https://react.dev/reference/react/ViewTransition)**: Animate elements that update inside a Transition or navigation
- **[`useEffectEvent`](https://react.dev/reference/react/useEffectEvent)**: Extract non-reactive logic from Effects into reusable Effect Event functions
- **[Activity](https://react.dev/reference/react/Activity)**: Render "background activity" by hiding UI with `display: none` while maintaining state and cleaning up Effects

## React Compiler Support

Built-in support for the React Compiler is now stable in **Next.js 16** following the React Compiler's 1.0 release. The React Compiler automatically memoizes components, reducing unnecessary re-renders with zero manual code changes.

## Caching APIs

New `"use cache"` directive replaces `unstable_cache`, `fetchCache`, and route segment configs like `revalidate`. The `cacheLife` and `cacheTag` APIs are stabilized (no longer need `unstable_` prefix).
```

--------------------------------

### Manually Install Next.js 16 and React

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/02-guides/upgrading/version-16.mdx

Manually update Next.js and React dependencies to their latest versions. Ensure @types/react and @types/react-dom are also updated if using TypeScript.

```bash
pnpm add next@latest react@latest react-dom@latest
```

```bash
npm install next@latest react@latest react-dom@latest
```

```bash
yarn add next@latest react@latest react-dom@latest
```

```bash
bun add next@latest react@latest react-dom@latest
```

--------------------------------

### Configure default Next.js scripts in package.json

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/08-turbopack.mdx

Turbopack is the default bundler in Next.js, requiring no additional configuration in your standard scripts.

```json
{
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "next dev",
    "build": "next build",
    "start": "next start"
  }
}
```

--------------------------------

### Configure Default Image Qualities in Next.js

Source: https://github.com/vercel/next.js/blob/canary/docs/01-app/03-api-reference/02-components/image.mdx

Specify the default allowed image quality values in `next.config.js`. This field is required starting with Next.js 16.

```js
module.exports = {
  images: {
    qualities: [75],
  },
}
```

